Subject: [PATCH] AP MLD: preserve the BSSID of the first secondary link

When the first link of an MLD is a secondary BSS, hostapd_setup_bss()
passes NULL as the address to hostapd_if_add() to reuse the MLD netdev.
The returned interface address then overwrites the configured link BSSID.
If 6 GHz starts before 5 GHz, both links can acquire the MLD netdev's
address and mac80211 rejects the second link with -EALREADY. The failed
setup also disables the ordinary BSS on that radio.

Save the interface address as the MLD address, but restore the configured
BSSID for the link. If no BSSID is configured, use the same random-link
address fallback as hostapd_driver_init(). Later links and non-MLD BSSes
are unchanged.

--- a/src/ap/hostapd.c
+++ b/src/ap/hostapd.c
@@ -1586,6 +1586,13 @@ int hostapd_setup_bss(struct hostapd_data *hapd, bool first,
 			os_memcpy(hapd->mld->mld_addr, hapd->own_addr,
 				  ETH_ALEN);
+
+			/* Keep the link BSSID separate from the shared MLD address. */
+			if (!is_zero_ether_addr(conf->bssid))
+				os_memcpy(hapd->own_addr, conf->bssid,
+					  ETH_ALEN);
+			else
+				random_mac_addr_keep_oui(hapd->own_addr);
 		}
 #endif /* CONFIG_IEEE80211BE */
 	}
 
